## Power BI Service

### 1. Workspace

A Workspace in Power BI Service is a collaborative environment where users can create, manage, and organize reports,
dashboards, datasets, and dataflows. It allows multiple team members to work together on the same project. Workspaces
provide role-based permissions such as Admin, Member, Contributor, and Viewer. They help organizations separate projects
and maintain security. Users can publish Power BI content from Power BI Desktop directly into a workspace.

**Example:**
A sales team creates a workspace named "Sales Analysis" where all sales reports are stored.

---

### 2. Dataflows

Dataflows are collections of data preparation processes used in Power BI Service. They help extract, transform, and
load (ETL) data from different sources into reusable datasets. Dataflows use Power Query for transforming data and 
can be shared across multiple reports. This reduces repeated work and improves efficiency. Data is stored in cloud
storage for easy access.

**Example:**
A company creates a customer dataflow and uses it in multiple reports.

---

### 3. Datasets

A Dataset is a collection of data imported or connected to Power BI. It acts as the data source for reports and dashboards. 
Datasets contain tables, relationships, calculations, and measures. Users can create multiple reports from a single dataset.
They support scheduled refresh for updated information.

**Example:**
A sales dataset contains customer, product, and sales transaction tables.

---

### 4. Reports

Reports are collections of visualizations created using datasets in Power BI. They can contain charts, graphs, maps, tables, 
and filters spread across multiple pages. Reports help users analyze business data interactively. Users can drill down and 
apply filters to view detailed information.

**Example:**
A report shows monthly sales performance using charts and graphs.

---

### 5. Dashboards

Dashboards are single-page visual summaries that combine important visuals from one or multiple reports. They provide a quick
overview of business performance. Dashboards can display real-time data and key metrics. Users can pin report visuals to 
dashboards for easy monitoring.

**Example:**
A dashboard displays total revenue, profit, and sales growth KPIs.

---

### 6. Sharing Reports

Power BI allows users to share reports with others inside or outside an organization. Shared reports help team members collaborate 
and access insights. Permissions control who can view or edit reports. Users can share through email links or workspaces.

**Example:**
A manager shares a sales report with team members.

---

### 7. Collaboration

Collaboration in Power BI enables multiple users to work together on reports and dashboards. Team members can comment, edit, and
share content. Workspaces support collaboration by assigning different roles to users. Collaboration improves productivity 
and decision-making.

**Example:**
Marketing and sales teams work together on a business analysis report.

---

### 8. Scheduled Refresh

Scheduled Refresh automatically updates datasets at specific intervals. It ensures reports and dashboards always display the
latest data. Users can configure refresh frequency such as daily or weekly. This reduces manual updates and improves 
accuracy.

**Example:**
A report refreshes every day at 9 AM automatically.

---

### 9. Gateway Configuration

A Gateway connects on-premises data sources to Power BI Service. It securely transfers data between local databases and 
the cloud. Gateways are necessary for scheduled refresh and live connections. There are Personal Gateway and Standard 
Gateway options.

**Example:**
A company database on a local server connects to Power BI using a gateway.

---

### 10. Row-Level Security (RLS)

Row-Level Security restricts users from viewing unauthorized data. It applies filters at the row level within datasets.
Different users can see different data based on assigned roles. RLS improves data security and privacy.

**Example:**
A regional manager sees only data related to their own region.
